1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:16,500
Now Leave of Absence, Readmission and AWOL. All three run on the same kind of board, so seeing one means seeing all three. First, a student asks for a leave.

2
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:32,699
Daniel Fernandez, a Master of Arts in Education student, signs in. The student accepted a work assignment in another city.

3
00:00:34,399 --> 00:00:49,000
Under Requests, the student opens Leave of Absence. The form shows the limits: two semesters at a time, renewable once, four semesters in total at most.

4
00:00:50,100 --> 00:01:11,000
'Rules applied on this screen' lists the same rules Graduate School staff applies, each with its Handbook page. Needs review marks prototype values the Graduate School must still confirm.

5
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:29,500
The student picks the start and end dates. The system says two semesters. The student adds a reason and short explanation.

6
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:40,399
Before sending, the student checks a summary: from, to, and the reason.

7
00:01:40,599 --> 00:01:53,500
Submitted. The request now waits for Graduate School staff review.

8
00:01:53,700 --> 00:02:01,299
Graduate School staff signs in.

9
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:19,400
Leave of Absence board: columns are stages, cards are requests. Graduate School staff finds Daniel's card in Submitted, flagged 'awaiting your review'.

10
00:02:22,599 --> 00:02:38,500
Graduate School staff drags the card to In staff review. Dragging equals the buttons, and the server checks every move, so no card moves where the rules forbid.

11
00:02:40,199 --> 00:02:47,800
Graduate School staff can read the same requests as a table.

12
00:02:50,400 --> 00:02:58,600
Graduate School staff opens the request: leave period, reason, and the student's explanation.

13
00:02:58,800 --> 00:03:14,300
The policy check below advises staff. Each rule shows Pass or Needs review; minimum residency shows Needs review, a prototype rule. The check never decides. The Dean does.

14
00:03:24,300 --> 00:03:33,300
Graduate School staff forwards it to the Dean, noting the checks.

15
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:45,100
The card moves to With the Dean, locked for staff. The board shows 'Waiting for: Dean', not a hidden card.

16
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:53,300
The Dean signs in.

17
00:03:58,500 --> 00:04:13,300
The Dean sees the same board, with requests awaiting decision.

18
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:27,300
The Dean drags the card to Leave scheduled. The approval window opens for a note the student will read.

19
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:40,800
Approved. The card sits in Leave scheduled, flagged for the Registrar. The Dean could instead return it for revision or deny it, with a comment.

20
00:04:41,899 --> 00:04:54,800
Graduate School staff signs in.

21
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:04,000
Graduate School staff sees the card in Leave scheduled. Normally the leave starts by itself on day one.

22
00:05:07,300 --> 00:05:17,199
For the demo, Graduate School staff drags the card to On leave now.

23
00:05:17,399 --> 00:05:24,600
Daniel is now on leave, and enrollment is closed for the student.

24
00:05:25,399 --> 00:05:38,300
Below is the Registrar list. The portal sends no e-mail. Graduate School staff downloads it, sends it through the official channel, marks it sent, then acknowledged.

25
00:05:40,300 --> 00:05:47,900
Graduate School staff downloads the list; the system notes no e-mail was sent.

26
00:05:51,500 --> 00:06:03,100
Graduate School staff ticks Daniel and marks the list sent to the Registrar, so the office knows what was handed over.

27
00:06:04,199 --> 00:06:22,199
Finally, Daniel signs back in to see the decision.

28
00:06:22,399 --> 00:06:31,800
On the student side, Daniel sees the status On leave and every message along the way.
